0030": "$\\Lambda \\to A$", "MATH_0031": "$\\mathfrak m_A$", "MATH_0032": "$C = \\Lambda[y_1, \\ldots, y_n]/(f_1, \\ldots, f_m)$", "MATH_0033": "$C \\to R$", "MATH_0034": "$R$", "MATH_0035": "$f_1 = \\ldots = f_m = 0$", "MATH_0036": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0037": "$R$", "MATH_0038": "$\\Lambda_{\\mathfrak m}$", "MATH_0039": "$\\Lambda_{\\mathfrak m}^h$", "MATH_0040": "$\\Lambda_{\\mathfrak m}^h$", "MATH_0041": "$\\Lambda_{\\mathfrak m}$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-approximate-versal}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{algebraic-section-split}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{lemma-approximate-versal}", "REF_0004": "\\ref{algebra-lemma-smooth-test-artinian}", "REF_0005": "\\ref{more-morphisms-lemma-slice-smooth}", "REF_0006": "\\ref{smoothing-section-approximation-G-rings}", "REF_0007": "\\ref{lemma-approximate-versal}", "REF_0008": "\\ref{smoothing-section-introduction}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\begin{enumerate}", "STRUCT_0002": "\\item", "STRUCT_0003": "\\item", "STRUCT_0004": "\\item", "STRUCT_0005": "\\end{enumerate}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{example}\n\\label{example-approximate-versal-implies}\n", "suffix": "\\end{example}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 2, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:2e607bb18379c23677f73701a772c8c74026231a7ce9ebd61d4ff48692e95c44", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:7a549440354c74dd7d82546fb2fe1b7057bf0f2e9b369311d796a90986bb2406", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:163d568efc6587dbb409e4f42805af31feaad18e2be4ea6c9ea246ef38111c2c", "source_text": "In this example we show that the local ring <MATH_0001> has to be\na G-ring in order for the result of Lemma <REF_0001> to\nbe true. Namely, let <MATH_0002> be a Noetherian ring and let <MATH_0003>\nbe a maximal ideal of <MATH_0004>. Set <MATH_0005>. Let\n<MATH_0006> be a factorization with <MATH_0007> of finite type over\n<MATH_0008>. Set <MATH_0009>, <MATH_0010>, and\n<MATH_0011>. Consider the functor\n<MATH_0012> defined by the rule\n<MATHSEG_0001>if <MATHSEG_0002> factors through <MATHSEG_0003>else<MATHSEG_0004>\nLet <MATH_0013> is the category fibred in sets associated\nto <MATH_0014>, see Algebraic Stacks, Section <REF_0002>.\nThen <MATH_0015> is limit preserving on objects and\nthere exists an effective, versal formal object <MATH_0016> over <MATH_0017>.\nHence if the conclusion of Lemma <REF_0003> holds\nfor <MATH_0018>, then there exists a finite type ring map <MATH_0019>\nand a maximal ideal <MATH_0020> lying over <MATH_0021> such that\n<STRUCT_0001>\n<STRUCT_0002> <MATH_0022>,\n<STRUCT_0003> <MATH_0023> and <MATH_0024> satisfy condition (4) of\nAlgebra, Lemma <REF_0004>, and\n<STRUCT_0004> there exists a <MATH_0025>-algebra map <MATH_0026>.\n<STRUCT_0005>\nThus <MATH_0027> is smooth at <MATH_0028> by the lemma cited.\nSlicing <MATH_0029> we may assume that <MATH_0030> is <ACCENT_0001>tale at\n<MATH_0031>, see for example\nMore on Morphisms, Lemma <REF_0005>\nor argue directly. Write <MATH_0032>.\nThen <MATH_0033> corresponds to a solution in <MATH_0034> of the system of equations\n<MATH_0035>, see Smoothing Ring Maps, Section\n<REF_0006>.\nThus if the conclusion of\nLemma <REF_0007> holds for every <MATH_0036> as\nabove, then a system of equations which has a solution in <MATH_0037> has a\nsolution in the henselization of <MATH_0038>.\nIn other words, the approximation property holds for\n<MATH_0039>. This implies that <MATH_0040>\nis a G-ring (insert future reference here; see also discussion in\nSmoothing Ring Maps, Section <REF_0008>)\nwhich in turn implies that <MATH_0041> is a G-ring.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:cbd91dd5c356128ce402213c921b94bab4b6ce0c2b07154d7f2cb941fd3960e4", "unit_id": "tag:07XI:statement"}
